RSX/MSL: cache translated shaders in memory and, with PS3RECOMP_MSL_CACHE=<dir>, on disk.

Every guest program a scene meets for the first time is translated HLSL -> SPIR-V (glslang, with the legalization
passes) -> MSL (spirv-cross) on the thread that walks the FIFO, 20-200 ms each (the Metal compile after it is cached
by the OS). In Resistance 3's opening cutscene that stalled the walker for 160-360 ms on the same frames of every run
(PS3_FTRACE: drain passes of 184-194 ms with no GPU wait or readback in them; `sample` of one: spvtools::opt), and
the woman's head jerked back and forth with a smeared motion-blur frame after each stall. rsx_hlsl_to_msl now keeps
what it translated, as rsx_hlsl_to_spirv already does for Vulkan: the key is the stage, the PS3RECOMP_MSL_FAST setting
and the whole HLSL text; with PS3RECOMP_MSL_CACHE=<existing directory> one file per shader (header with glslang and
spirv-cross versions and the translator's identity; a mismatch or a damaged file is a miss and is rewritten). The
identity is a hash of rsx_shader_msl.cpp/.h that CMake passes in (PS3RECOMP_MSL_TRANSLATOR_ID, both files are
configure dependencies), not the build time the SPIR-V cache uses: build_host.sh rebuilds the toolkit from a clean tree
whenever a patch changes, and a build-time stamp threw the whole cache away each time (the first run after it juddered
again).
host/main.cpp sets PS3RECOMP_MSL_CACHE=./cache/msl.

Test: libs/video/tests/test_shader_msl.c, translation cache section (memory hit, disk hit across a reset, damaged and
truncated files). Scripted run, the cutscene from the comic skip on: walker passes over 80 ms, 11 with an empty
cache (288 shaders written), 2 with it warm (both on the loading screen before the cutscene); the head no longer
jerks (checked by eye).

Upstream: not proposed yet.

---

